> Why the row operations certify a greatest common divisor

For any integer quotient q, a common divisor of r_old and r also divides
r_old−q·r, and the reverse implication follows by adding q·r back.
Thus the set of common divisors is unchanged. A nonzero divisor produces a
remainder whose absolute value is smaller; repeated division terminates.
The last nonzero remainder has the gcd's magnitude.

Track two coefficient rows. Initially, 1·a+0·b=a and 0·a+1·b=b.
When q=old_r/r is the actual truncating quotient, update

\[
r'=r_{old}-qr,\qquad s'=s_{old}-qs,\qquad t'=t_{old}-qt.
\]

Linearity preserves r'=s'a+t'b. At termination, negate the surviving
remainder and both coefficients if that remainder is negative. The returned
gcd is nonnegative and its coefficients satisfy a·x+b·y=gcd.
The consecutive coefficient rows also retain determinant +1 or −1;
each subtract-and-swap step reverses its sign, which tests their integer
unimodular structure independently of a plotted remainder curve.

The core's certificate checker verifies both the identity and that the
claimed positive gcd divides both input magnitudes. If d divides a and b,
then d divides a·x+b·y=g; together with g being a common divisor this
certifies greatestness. An identity alone is insufficient: 6·1+10·0=6
does not make six a common divisor of six and ten. For two zero inputs,
gcd zero is the explicit convention. Actual legacy coefficients are (1,0),
correcting the old comment that promised (0,0).

The implementation retains input order and C++ truncation toward zero,
including an initial quotient zero when needed. It is a signed-input
variant of the usual nonnegative ordered presentation. The Handbook of
Applied Cryptography's algorithms 2.107 and 2.142 supply the standard
extended-gcd and inverse construction; their input conventions differ
from this native signed interface.

> Retain the original results and the 100,000-pair check

The original compiled result is ExtendedGcd{2,−9,47}. Its three signed
int64 fields and compiled signature remain. The original seeded random
check below still uses 100,000 pairs in [−100000,100000]. That range is
safe for std::gcd's signed-magnitude precondition. The native certificate
checker handles the Bezout products without a report-local Euclid loop.

The book's example 2.109, (4864,3458), supplies a second known answer:
38=32·4864−45·3458. Counts distinguish it from the original pair and
show that reversed input order can add a genuine zero-quotient division.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/euclid_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const auto original=dm::algorithms::extended_gcd(240,46);std::print("Legacy worked result: gcd {}; x {}; y {}; inverse(3,11) {}.\n",original.gcd,original.x,original.y,dm::algorithms::modular_inverse(3,11));
  std::mt19937_64 rng(20240927);std::uniform_int_distribution<std::int64_t> dist(-100000,100000);std::size_t good=0;
  for(int i=0;i<100000;++i){const auto a=dist(rng),b=dist(rng);const auto e=dm::algorithms::extended_gcd(a,b);const dm::algorithms::ExtendedGcdCertificate c{static_cast<std::uint64_t>(e.gcd),e.x,e.y};if(e.gcd==std::gcd(a,b)&&dm::algorithms::bezout_certificate_valid(a,b,c))++good;}
  std::print("Original randomized check: {}/100000 pairs satisfy the Bezout certificate and match std::gcd.\n\n",good);if(good!=100000)throw std::runtime_error("original randomized check failed");
  std::vector<double> counts;std::vector<std::string> labels;std::print("| a | b | gcd | x | y | actual divisions | captured rows |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& [a,b]:std::vector<std::pair<std::int64_t,std::int64_t>>{{240,46},{4864,3458},{46,240},{-240,46},{0,0}}){const auto t=dm::algorithms::extended_gcd_trace(a,b);const auto& c=t.outcome.certificate;std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",a,b,c.gcd,c.x,c.y,t.outcome.work.divisions,t.captured_entries);counts.push_back(t.outcome.work.divisions);labels.push_back(std::format("{},{}",a,b));}
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(counts,labels,"actual divisions",{37,99,235});p.title("Actual Euclidean division counts").x_label("Input pair in its supplied order").y_label("Quotient/remainder iterations").y_limits(0,7).size(1000,530).save_svg("build/euclid_original.svg");
}
