> From the same-pass certificate to an inverse

If a·x+m·y=1, reducing modulo m gives a·x congruent to one.
Conversely, an inverse implies the gcd divides one, so coprimality is
necessary and sufficient. Reduce the actual coefficient x into [0,m).
The implementation first takes x%m, then adds m only if that remainder
is negative, avoiding an overflowing unreduced x+m expression.

modular_inverse_trace captures the actual Euclid rows during that inverse
computation, rather than reconstructing them afterward. Positive m is
validated before history admission. A nonunit throws when its gcd is
known; use an ordinary gcd certificate to inspect that failure separately.
The legacy API permits m=1 and returns its sole residue zero, including
for a=0 or INT64_MIN. In that degenerate modulus, one and zero are
congruent; it should not be checked by demanding a raw remainder of one.

For 3 modulo 11, four modular additions of three reach residue one.
The circle below is a conceptual residue check using the existing safe
modular-multiplication API. The Euclidean trace, not the circle, computes
the inverse. No claim about cryptographic constant-time execution is made.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/euclid_inverse.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");std::print("| a | m | gcd | inverse or nonunit | coefficient x | coefficient y | actual divisions |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& [a,m]:std::vector<std::pair<std::int64_t,std::int64_t>>{{3,11},{-3,11},{7,26},{0,1},{std::numeric_limits<std::int64_t>::min(),1},{6,9}}){try{const auto t=dm::algorithms::modular_inverse_trace(a,m);const auto& c=t.euclid.outcome.certificate;const __int128 residue=((static_cast<__int128>(a)*t.inverse)%m+m)%m;if(residue!=1%m)throw std::runtime_error("inverse certificate failed");std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",a,m,c.gcd,t.inverse,c.x,c.y,t.euclid.outcome.work.divisions);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){const auto e=dm::algorithms::extended_gcd_work(a,m);std::print("| {} | {} | {} | nonunit | {} | {} | {} |\n",a,m,e.certificate.gcd,e.certificate.x,e.certificate.y,e.work.divisions);}}
  const auto inverse=dm::algorithms::modular_inverse_trace(3,11);dm::graphics::Diagram g(64,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=1.1;box.height=1.1;box.circle=true;box.font_size=23;
  std::print("\nResidues for 3*k modulo 11, k=0..10:");
  for(std::uint64_t k=0;k<11;++k){const auto r=dm::algorithms::mul_mod(3,k,11);std::print(" {}",r);const double angle=std::numbers::pi/2-2*std::numbers::pi*k/11;box.fill=k==static_cast<std::uint64_t>(inverse.inverse)?dm::graphics::RGB{254,215,170}:dm::graphics::RGB{191,219,254};g.node(std::to_string(k),{4*std::cos(angle),4*std::sin(angle)},std::to_string(r),box);}
  for(int k=0;k<11;++k)g.edge(std::to_string(k),std::to_string((k+1)%11),"+3");g.text({0,6},"Four modular additions of three reach one",25).text({0,-6},std::format("Actual inverse: {}; Euclid divisions: {}; captured rows: {}",inverse.inverse,inverse.euclid.outcome.work.divisions,inverse.euclid.captured_entries),22).save_svg("build/euclid_inverse.svg");std::print("\nActual inverse trace: inverse {}; divisions {}; rows {}.\n",inverse.inverse,inverse.euclid.outcome.work.divisions,inverse.euclid.captured_entries);
}
