> Division counts, Fibonacci inputs and bit complexity

Remainder magnitudes are nonnegative and decrease until zero. Over two
successive positive remainder reductions, the magnitude falls by at least a
factor of two: either the next remainder is already at most half the current
one, or the following subtraction leaves less than half. This gives a
logarithmic division count. Zero inputs are constant-work cases; an initial
zero quotient can add one division when input order is reversed.

Consecutive Fibonacci numbers approach the slowest shrinkage because most
quotients are one. The native counts below include the terminal zero step.
F_92=7540113804746346429 and F_91=4660046610375530309 fit signed int64;
they require ninety divisions in that order and ninety-one in reverse.
A similarly wide input paired with one needs only one division, showing
that bit width alone does not determine a particular run's work.

These are fixed-word operation counts on int64 inputs with signed128-bit
intermediates. They are not an O(log n) bit-operation claim for unbounded
integers: larger divisions have their own cost, and the Handbook gives a
quadratic logarithmic bit bound for its basic arithmetic model. Ordinary
calls use constant auxiliary state. An owned trace stores C=2+D whole row
records for D divisions; metadata, capacities, allocator bytes and runtime
temporaries are excluded, so C is not peak memory.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/euclid_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");std::vector<std::int64_t> f{0,1};const auto maximum=std::numeric_limits<std::int64_t>::max();while(f.back()<=maximum-f[f.size()-2])f.push_back(f.back()+f[f.size()-2]);std::vector<double> n,divisions,rows;
  std::print("| Fibonacci n | F_n | F_(n-1) | actual divisions | coefficient updates | captured rows |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=3;i<f.size();++i){const auto t=dm::algorithms::extended_gcd_trace(f[i],f[i-1]);if(t.outcome.work.divisions!=i-2||t.captured_entries!=t.outcome.work.divisions+2)throw std::runtime_error("Fibonacci division/row identity failed");n.push_back(i);divisions.push_back(t.outcome.work.divisions);rows.push_back(t.captured_entries);if(i==5||i==10||i==20||i==40||i==60||i==80||i==92)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,f[i],f[i-1],t.outcome.work.divisions,t.outcome.work.coefficient_updates,t.captured_entries);}
  const auto forward=dm::algorithms::extended_gcd_work(f.back(),f[f.size()-2]),reverse=dm::algorithms::extended_gcd_work(f[f.size()-2],f.back()),one=dm::algorithms::extended_gcd_work(maximum,1);
  std::print("\nLargest displayed Fibonacci pair: forward {} divisions; reversed {} divisions; INT64_MAX with one {} division.\n",forward.work.divisions,reverse.work.divisions,one.work.divisions);
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.line(n,divisions,"Actual divisions D",{37,99,235},2).line(n,rows,"Captured rows D+2",{124,58,237},2).title("Consecutive Fibonacci inputs: measured work and history").x_label("Fibonacci index n").y_label("Actual divisions or whole row records").size(1000,530).save_svg("build/euclid_work.svg");
}
