// result_end
\caption(The unsigned gcd, widened quotient and terminal coefficient all equal 2^63 in different actual executions. Safe certificates remain available when the legacy gcd field cannot hold the mathematical value.)
\alttext(Three native boxes show the actual unsigned gcd, quotient and terminal coefficient 9223372036854775808, explaining their different roles and the legacy storage boundary.)

> References

- Menezes, van Oorschot and Vanstone (1996), *Handbook of Applied Cryptography*,
  CRC Press, chapter 2, algorithms 2.104, 2.107 and 2.142; example 2.109.
  [Official chapter](https://cacr.uwaterloo.ca/hac/about/chap2.pdf).
  The signed native variant retains input order, truncating quotients,
  final normalisation and checked fixed-width result ranges.
- C++ working draft, [numeric.ops.gcd](https://eel.is/c%2B%2Bdraft/numeric.ops.gcd),
  representable-magnitude precondition. Its unsigned-input form is used in
  native boundary tests; the original small signed random fixture is safe.

